\chapter{DISCUSSION}

This chapter interprets the experimental findings in depth: what the results mean for our two research questions, how they situate within prior work, why key design decisions were made, and what limitations and future directions follow.

\section{Revisiting Research Questions}

\subsection{RQ1: Does the Dissonance-Aware Framework Outperform the Baselines?}

Our first hypothesis (H1) predicted that the Dissonance-Aware therapist would achieve the highest or tied-highest scores on all seven evaluation metrics. On GPT-4o mini this prediction is met in full: Dissonance-Aware attains the highest mean on every metric, with significant paired gains over Baseline on the CTRS craft dimensions (Guided Discovery $+0.24$, $t = 3.1$; Focus $+0.34$, $t = 4.7$; Strategy $+0.17$, $t = 2.7$; total-score $t = 3.53$) and pairwise confirmation (net win-rate $0.680$, sign-test $p = 0.0001$).

The most consequential finding of this experiment, however, is not the size of the dissonance gain but its \emph{source}. The Multimodal (Vocal-Aware) condition --- identical vocal inputs, identical VA values, identical descriptors, minus the dissonance computation and its interpretive framing --- did not merely fail to improve over Baseline; on both weaker backbones it \emph{regressed significantly below} it (GPT-4o mini: total $-1.78$, $t = -4.75$; Qwen: $t = -6.13$; per-metric, Guided Discovery drops $-0.51$ and $-0.44$ respectively, $t = -7.1$ and $-7.9$). Dissonance-Aware then recovers and exceeds Baseline by a wide margin over Multimodal (GPT-4o mini: total $+2.75$, $t = 7.94$; Guided Discovery $+0.75$, $t = 10.1$; pairwise net win-rate $0.800$, $p < 10^{-4}$, with dissonance transcripts on average 1,310 characters \emph{shorter}, excluding verbosity explanations).

This inverts the reading of our earlier stateless-client pilot, where vocal metadata appeared to drive most of the gain and the dissonance computation appeared additive at best. With stateful clients, the picture is mechanistically sharper: raw vocal metadata injected into a therapist prompt is \emph{noise that a weaker model acts on} --- producing tone-referencing but surface-level responses that blind judges penalize --- while the dissonance framing (continuous deltas plus explicit guidance to treat large mismatch as something to explore gently) is what converts the same numbers into deeper session craft. The dissonance computation is the active ingredient; the vocal channel is its raw material.

Two further observations complete the RQ1 picture. First, Emotion (Text-Only) is indistinguishable from Baseline on GPT-4o mini ($t = -0.03$) and significantly worse on Qwen ($t = -3.19$): appending VA numbers without interpretive structure does not help and can hurt, validating the staged condition design as a genuine information ladder rather than a formality. Second, the binary dissonance flag fires rarely under synthetic speech (0.8\% of turns on GPT-4o mini; 0.9--3.7\% across backbones), yet the Dissonance--Multimodal gap is large --- so the effect cannot be carried by flagged turns alone. The mechanism is the always-on delta framing, with the flag acting as an intensifier at genuine mismatch moments. The real-voice demonstration (Section~\ref{sec:chat-demo}) verifies the flagged path end-to-end: a genuine $\delta_V = -1.05$ triggers precisely the targeted, non-accusatory exploration the framework prescribes.

\subsection{RQ2: Is the Advantage Robust Across LLM Backbones?}

Our second hypothesis (H2) predicted a consistent, architecture-independent advantage. The honest verdict is \emph{partially supported, with a sharper robust core}. The Dissonance-over-Baseline advantage is significant on two of four backbones --- GPT-4o mini (Layer 2 $t = 3.53$; Layer 3 $p = 0.0001$) and DeepSeek V3.2 (Layer 2 $t = 2.18$; Layer 3 $p = 0.00004$) --- neutral on Qwen (all three layers converge: $t = -1.90$ n.s., pairwise $p = 0.649$, both judges leaning Baseline on the sampled subset), and unmeasurable on Claude, where every condition saturates the scale. What \emph{is} architecture-robust is the ordering against the other information conditions: Dissonance-Aware never loses significantly to any condition on any backbone, and it beats Multimodal significantly wherever the instruments can discriminate (GPT-4o mini, Qwen), with the cross-family second judge concurring directionally in every committed cell.

The DeepSeek cell deserves emphasis as a methodological finding in its own right. On absolute scores its four conditions sit within a 0.03-point band --- the judge returns literally identical scores for most sessions --- and the naive reading would be ``no effect.'' The paired total-score test already detects a significant advantage ($t = 2.18$), and the swap-consistent pairwise layer resolves it decisively (46 wins, 40 ties, 14 losses; $p = 0.00004$). A real preference existed that the absolute instrument could not express. This is a concrete replication, within a single study, of the judge-generosity misalignment documented by Haydarov et al.\ \cite{haydarov-etal-2025-towards}, and it is the strongest justification for the three-layer evaluation design.

The Claude cell is the mirror image: the GPT-4o judge declares 99--100\% ties with near-zero position consistency (its verdicts flip under order swap), meaning the sessions are perceptually equivalent at its resolution; the absolute-scale nominal winner (Multimodal, 5.24 vs.\ 5.09) rests on differences of one or two scale points per session in a cell where the judge assigned identical scores to 100/100 sampled sessions. The cross-family second judge, which does commit on the same transcripts, preferred Dissonance over Multimodal 10-to-2. We read Claude as \emph{inconclusive at ceiling}, consistent with MIRROR's finding that strong backbones leave little headroom for emotional metadata \cite{kim-etal-2025-mirror}, rather than as evidence against the framework.

The practical pattern that survives across all four backbones is a \emph{headroom-gated} benefit: the dissonance signal helps measurably where the judge can still discriminate quality (GPT-4o mini, DeepSeek), is neutral where the text-only therapist is already strong (Qwen's Baseline Guided Discovery of 4.50 is the highest baseline CTRS-G score in the study), and is unmeasurable where everything saturates (Claude). The deployment implication is unchanged in spirit but sharper in form: the framework's value concentrates exactly in the cost-constrained regime --- GPT-4o mini under Dissonance-Aware reaches Understanding 4.98 against Claude's unaided Baseline of 5.13, a 0.15-point gap at a small fraction of the inference cost.

\subsection{What We Did Not Achieve}

Three goals remain unmet. First, we did not establish universal Dissonance-over-Baseline superiority: Qwen is a genuine neutral case across all three layers and both judges, and we report it as such rather than averaging it away. Second, our evaluation remains LLM-judged: cross-family agreement is only fair ($\kappa = 0.217$), rescued at the directional level (6/6 committed cells agree) but far from the reliability of trained human raters. Third, our dialogues remain synthetic: client personas are simulated, client speech is TTS-rendered, and true masked affect --- the clinical phenomenon the framework targets --- is approximated rather than observed.

\section{Comparing with Past Work}

Table~\ref{tab:past-work} positions our framework against the closest prior works, in terms of the cues each system uses, the task it addresses, and how it is evaluated.

\begin{table}[hbt!]
\caption{Comparison with prior work: cues, tasks, and evaluation}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lp{2.6cm}p{2.2cm}p{2.6cm}p{3.4cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Work} & \textbf{Cues Used} & \textbf{Task} & \textbf{Evaluation} & \textbf{Key Difference from Ours} \\ \hline
CACTUS \cite{lee2024cactus} & Text only & CBT dialogue generation & CTRS-based COUNSELINGEVAL & No vocal channel at all \\
MIRROR \cite{kim-etal-2025-mirror} & Text + facial expression & CBT reframing with resistance & GPT judge (skills 0--6, alliance 1--5) + expert pairwise & Visual modality only; no voice \\
Haydarov et al.\ \cite{haydarov-etal-2025-towards} & Face-to-speech VA dissonance & Therapeutic intervention generation & LLM judge + expert study & Dissonance between face and voice, not text and voice; single-turn interventions \\
Wang \& Wu \cite{wang2024evaluationdatainconsistencymultimodal} & Text + audio inconsistency & Multimodal sentiment analysis & DiffEmo benchmark accuracy & Treats mismatch as a failure mode, not a signal \\
SpeechCueLLM \cite{wu2025beyond} & Prosody as text descriptors & Emotion recognition & F1 (IEMOCAP, MELD) & Classification, not dialogue generation \\
Sun et al.\ \cite{sun2024beyond} & Pitch, loudness, duration & Robot navigation decisions & Winning rate vs.\ text-only & Non-therapeutic domain \\
\textbf{Ours} & \textbf{Text-to-speech VA delta + flag + vocal descriptors} & \textbf{CBT therapist response generation} & \textbf{7-metric blind judge + paired $t$ + swap-consistent pairwise + cross-judge $\kappa$, 4 backbones} & Dissonance as a prompt-level therapeutic signal \\ \hline
\end{tabular}%
}
\label{tab:past-work}
\end{table}

Our results extend four threads of prior findings, and complicate a fifth. First, Sun et al.\ and Wu et al.\ (SpeechCueLLM) showed that vocal features injected as text improve LLM decision-making and emotion recognition in classification-style tasks; our Multimodal condition shows that the same injection strategy \emph{does not transfer automatically to generative therapeutic dialogue} --- without interpretive framing it degraded weaker therapist backbones below their text-only performance. This is, to our knowledge, the first documented case of vocal-feature injection backfiring in therapy generation, and it sharpens rather than contradicts the prior work: the value of vocal cues depends on giving the model a reasoning frame for them. Second, MIRROR demonstrated that non-verbal cues improve AI therapists' handling of client resistance, validated with expert pairwise comparisons, and documented GPT-evaluator length bias; we adopt both instruments (pairwise preference with win-rate reporting, length auditing) and replicate its ceiling finding for strong backbones. Third, Wang and Wu documented that multimodal models degrade when text and audio conflict; our results supply the constructive complement: the same conflict, computed explicitly and framed therapeutically, becomes an asset --- the Dissonance$-$Multimodal gap is the largest significant effect in the study. Fourth, the closest work, Haydarov et al., who --- inspired by Hochschild's concept of emotional labor \cite{hochschild1983managed} --- compute emotional dissonance between facial and vocal emotion and show that dissonance-guided prompting improves generated interventions; we converge on the same conclusion through the text-to-speech channel pair --- the two channels most available in real counseling deployments (teletherapy with cameras off) --- and additionally show the effect's boundary conditions across four backbones. The complication is their evaluation finding that LLM judges misalign with human experts in this domain: our $\kappa = 0.217$ across judge families suggests the misalignment extends to judge-to-judge agreement, which is why no conclusion in this thesis rests on a single instrument.

\section{Experimental Decisions}

Several design choices shaped the results and deserve explicit discussion.

\textbf{Prompt-level feature injection instead of fine-tuning.} All four therapist backbones remain frozen; emotional information reaches the model purely through the prompt. This decision is what makes the architecture-agnostic claim (H2) testable at all, since the identical signal can be injected into any LLM without modification. It also makes the framework cheap to replicate: no training runs, no adaptation cost, and immediate transfer to future models. The trade-off surfaced concretely in the results: prompt-based signals compete for the model's attention with dialogue content, and weaker backbones (GPT-4o mini, Qwen) demonstrably mishandle raw vocal metadata --- the Multimodal regression --- unless the dissonance frame tells them what the numbers mean.

\textbf{Fixed threshold $\tau = 0.5$.} We deliberately kept the dissonance threshold fixed and simple, following prior work, rather than learning or tuning it. A fixed, interpretable rule keeps the experimental variable isolated: any performance difference is attributable to the signal's presence, not to threshold optimization. The cost is rigidity; a single global threshold cannot distinguish a genuinely masked client from a noisy TTS artifact. That the framework's gains survive with the flag firing on only $\sim$1\% of turns (Section 5.1.2) indicates the continuous deltas and their framing carry the effect, and it makes adaptive, context-aware thresholding a natural future direction rather than a fix for a broken mechanism.

\textbf{Resist persona for the client.} The client LLM was configured with skepticism and partial resistance, inspired by the MIRROR framework's emphasis on resistance handling. This persona creates precisely the conversational moments where dissonance is clinically meaningful: a client who says they are fine while sounding otherwise.

\textbf{Stateful client memory.} Relative to the stateless pilot, each simulated client conditions on its own prior utterances (role-reversed history), which produces more coherent, realistic sessions --- and, not incidentally, raised absolute scores by roughly one scale point across \emph{all} conditions, compressing the measurable headroom. This side effect is itself informative: it forced the evaluation to mature from single-layer absolute grading to the paired and pairwise layers, without which the DeepSeek advantage would have remained invisible.

\textbf{Shared problem seeds.} Every condition and every backbone ran on the identical set of 100 problem seeds, with comparisons paired at the seed level. Because clients are simulated interactively, client utterances diverge stochastically across conditions; the seed pairing is what removes scenario-level confounding and makes per-dialogue paired statistics legitimate.

\textbf{Three-layer blind evaluation with pre-registered criteria.} Automated evaluation was necessary to score 1,600 sessions and 800 pairwise comparisons, and a deterministic, condition-blind judge maximizes consistency. The swap-consistency rule, the length audit, the cross-family second judge, and the interpretation criteria fixed before the pairwise run were all adopted precisely because LLM judges are known to be generous, position-sensitive, and length-sensitive in this domain \cite{haydarov-etal-2025-towards,zheng2023judging,kim-etal-2025-mirror}. An LLM judge is still not a substitute for human clinical experts; our results should be read as strong but preliminary evidence of therapeutic quality.

\textbf{Synthetic speech via Zonos.} Using TTS-synthesized client audio avoids all privacy and ethics concerns around real patient recordings, and it gives exact control over intended vocal emotion. The cost is fidelity: the alpha-stage engine occasionally produces degenerate or truncated audio (mitigated in the final runs by a Director-output repair chain with neutral-prosody fallback), and its prosodic range is narrower than real human speech, which suppresses natural text--voice mismatch and keeps flag rates low.

\textbf{Stateful therapist context.} The therapist LLM receives the complete conversation history accumulated up to that turn, reflecting real therapeutic practice in which each response builds on earlier disclosures. The principal trade-off is a modest increase in prompt length as the session progresses, partially offset by allowing deeper, more contextually grounded responses.

\textbf{Generation temperature 0.7.} We selected 0.7 as the balance between natural conversational tone and topical stability for therapist role-play, and held it constant across all backbones and conditions to prevent temperature from becoming a confound.

\textbf{Implications for practitioners.} Read together, these decisions suggest a practical guideline for building emotionally aware therapeutic AI on a budget: you do not need a frontier model or a training pipeline --- but you also should not simply paste vocal features into the prompt. The critical ingredients are the mismatch computation itself and a prompt that frames mismatch as something to explore gently rather than to accuse; without that frame, the same features measurably degrade weaker models.

\section{Limitations and Future Work}

\subsection{Limitations}

We focus on concrete limitations that could affect the validity of our findings.

\textbf{TTS fidelity.} Our synthesized client audio comes from an alpha-stage TTS engine. Degenerate outputs and truncated sentences, though rare after the repair-and-fallback chain, inject noise into the speech VA and vocal descriptors; results obtained with production-grade TTS or real recordings could differ.

\textbf{Rare dissonance flags under synthetic speech.} Because the TTS director derives vocal emotion from the text content, speech VA tracks text VA closely and the flag fired on only 0.8--3.7\% of turns across backbones. The always-on delta framing carries the measured effect, but the flag's specific causal contribution at genuine mismatch moments remains verified only at demonstration scale (Section~\ref{sec:chat-demo}). A controlled manipulation synthesizing voices that deliberately contradict the text would settle it.

\textbf{Vocal descriptor variance dependency.} The librosa descriptors are only as variable as the client's emotional state. In the final runs the pitch component remained constant across all turns in all sampled dialogues (always ``very high pitch'' under the current Zonos voice configuration), while loudness and speaking rate varied. A descriptor with no turn-level variance cannot inform turn-level decisions, reducing the effective richness of the vocal channel.

\textbf{Judge reliability.} Cross-family inter-judge agreement is fair at best (overall $\kappa = 0.217$), driven by divergent tie-versus-commit styles and by genuinely indistinguishable content at ceiling; directional agreement (6/6 committed cells) is reassuring but coarser than item-level reliability. The primary judge also shares a model family with one backbone (GPT-4o judging GPT-4o mini sessions), a self-enhancement exposure that the second-judge design mitigates but does not eliminate. Absolute-scale saturation on Claude and DeepSeek means some true differences may be invisible to every automated instrument used here.

\textbf{Backbone-dependent benefit.} Dissonance-Aware does not beat Baseline on Qwen (neutral across all layers) and cannot be shown to on Claude (ceiling). The framework's benefit is headroom-gated, and a deployment decision should account for the strength of the intended backbone's text-only therapist.

\textbf{Computational overhead.} The Dissonance-Aware pipeline costs approximately 29\% more time per dialogue than Baseline (97.3s vs.\ 75.4s on Claude), driven mainly by longer therapist responses and by the WavLM and librosa extraction steps. Acceptable offline; relevant for real-time deployment.

\textbf{LLM-judged, synthetic, English-only data.} All evidence comes from synthetic dialogues evaluated by LLM judges. We have no evidence yet from real therapy clients or human expert raters, and the framework has not been tested in languages other than English or on long-term multi-session therapy.

\subsection{Future Work}

\textbf{Human expert validation.} The highest-priority extension is a MIRROR-style expert study \cite{kim-etal-2025-mirror}: a stratified subset of the existing pairwise comparisons (e.g., 50--200 per backbone) rated by trained CBT clinicians, yielding human--judge $\kappa$ and, if the direction holds, upgrading the central claims from ``LLM-judged'' to ``expert-corroborated.''

\textbf{Cross-turn dissonance tracking with adaptive threshold.} Tracking dissonance across turns rather than per utterance would enable detection of persistent hidden-affect patterns; an adaptive threshold that tightens after repeated flags addresses the rigidity of the fixed $\tau = 0.5$ rule while reducing false alarms from transient noise.

\textbf{Controlled dissonance manipulation.} Synthesizing client voices whose emotional delivery deliberately contradicts the text (e.g., cheerful delivery of distressed content) would raise the flag rate to a designed level and provide a direct causal test of the flag mechanism, complementing the always-on framing effect measured here.

\textbf{Real-voice deployment.} Replaying the framework with real recorded speech (as piloted in the demonstration session, where a real voice produced a clean $\delta_V = -1.05$ flag) would test whether the gains survive contact with real human affect.

Longer-term directions include disentangling shared, unique, and synergistic information across text and speech to reduce false alarms, and therapeutically reframed omission analysis on flagged turns --- adapting text-forensic reasoning into gentle exploration of what a client may be leaving unsaid, strictly as an exploration aid and never as deception detection.
